prompt_messages.content:
GOAL:
{'id': 'ir-10-accidental-deletion', 'description': 'Accidental data loss response: a developer accidentally dropped a production PostgreSQL table containing 10M rows of customer data. Must: immediately stop all writes to the database to prevent overwriting WAL, identify the exact deletion timestamp from logs, restore the database to a point-in-time (PITR) just before deletion, reconcile any data created after the restore point (re-apply from application logs), verify data integrity, and implement preventive controls (table-level drop protection, stricter RBAC). Risk factors: WAL retention window expiring before restore, data created after deletion point being lost, PITR restore overwriting other concurrent changes. Tools available: pg_basebackup, wal-g, aws RDS, psql, ansible.', 'constraints': {'budget': {'max_tokens': None, 'max_calls': None, 'max_revisions': None}, 'time': None, 'environment': 'production', 'tools': ['pg_basebackup', 'wal-g', 'aws RDS', 'psql']}, 'risk_tolerance': 'balanced', 'replan_policy': 'patch', 'approval_ttl': None, 'metadata': {}}

Produce the PlanVersion JSON now. version=1.

prompt_full_text:
--- system ---
/no_think You are a planner. Reply with ONLY a JSON object (no markdown, no prose, no thinking). Use EXACTLY these field names: id, goal_id, version, tasks, dependencies, branches. Each task uses: id, description, action, target, risk_class, optional rollback{trigger,action,safety_guard}, optional verification{what,how,expected}, optional preconditions (array of {description, fact, established_by}), optional parallel_group, optional blast_radius. preconditions MUST be objects with description, fact, established_by fields — NEVER strings. established_by MUST be the id of an earlier task in the plan that establishes the fact, or 'env' if it is an environment fact (not a task output). Do NOT use fact names or descriptions as established_by — use the task id. Each dependency uses: from_task, to_task, kind, optional reason. Each branch uses: id, kind (MUST be 'fan_out' or 'fan_in'), tasks (array of task id STRINGS, not objects), join (MUST be 'all', 'any', or 'quorum'). branches is OPTIONAL — omit it if the plan has no fan-out/fan-in structure. Do NOT put rollback or verification inside branches. High/critical risk tasks MUST have rollback AND verification. All tasks with risk_class high or critical MUST include both rollback and verification objects. Example shape: {"id":"plan-x","goal_id":"g","version":1,"tasks":[{"id":"backup","description":"Back up the database","action":"backup","target":"db","risk_class":"medium","preconditions":[{"description":"db is reachable","fact":"db_healthy","established_by":"env"}]},{"id":"migrate","description":"Apply schema migration","action":"migrate","target":"schema","risk_class":"high","rollback":{"trigger":"migration fails","action":"restore from backup","safety_guard":"verify backup integrity"},"verification":{"what":"schema version","how":"run checks","expected":"v2"}}],"dependencies":[{"from_task":"backup","to_task":"migrate","kind":"hard"}]}

--- user ---
GOAL:
{'id': 'ir-10-accidental-deletion', 'description': 'Accidental data loss response: a developer accidentally dropped a production PostgreSQL table containing 10M rows of customer data. Must: immediately stop all writes to the database to prevent overwriting WAL, identify the exact deletion timestamp from logs, restore the database to a point-in-time (PITR) just before deletion, reconcile any data created after the restore point (re-apply from application logs), verify data integrity, and implement preventive controls (table-level drop protection, stricter RBAC). Risk factors: WAL retention window expiring before restore, data created after deletion point being lost, PITR restore overwriting other concurrent changes. Tools available: pg_basebackup, wal-g, aws RDS, psql, ansible.', 'constraints': {'budget': {'max_tokens': None, 'max_calls': None, 'max_revisions': None}, 'time': None, 'environment': 'production', 'tools': ['pg_basebackup', 'wal-g', 'aws RDS', 'psql']}, 'risk_tolerance': 'balanced', 'replan_policy': 'patch', 'approval_ttl': None, 'metadata': {}}

Produce the PlanVersion JSON now. version=1.

response_content:
{
  "id": "ir-10-accidental-deletion-plan",
  "goal_id": "ir-10-accidental-deletion",
  "version": 1,
  "tasks": [
    {
      "id": "stop-writes",
      "description": "Stop all writes to the PostgreSQL database",
      "action": "stop",
      "target": "db",
      "risk_class": "high",
      "rollback": {
        "trigger": "failed to stop writes",
        "action": "restart application services",
        "safety_guard": "verify application is stopped"
      },
      "verification": {
        "what": "write operations status",
        "how": "check application logs",
        "expected": "no write operations"
      },
      "preconditions": [
        {
          "description": "database is reachable",
          "fact": "db_healthy",
          "established_by": "env"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "identify-deletion-timestamp",
      "description": "Identify the exact deletion timestamp from logs",
      "action": "analyze",
      "target": "logs",
      "risk_class": "medium",
      "preconditions": [
        {
          "description": "logs are accessible",
          "fact": "logs_accessible",
          "established_by": "stop-writes"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "pitr-restore",
      "description": "Restore the database to a point-in-time just before deletion",
      "action": "restore",
      "target": "db",
      "risk_class": "critical",
      "rollback": {
        "trigger": "PITR restore fails",
        "action": "restore from last backup",
        "safety_guard": "verify backup integrity"
      },
      "verification": {
        "what": "database state",
        "how": "check database integrity",
        "expected": "database state prior to deletion"
      },
      "preconditions": [
        {
          "description": "deletion timestamp is identified",
          "fact": "deletion_timestamp",
          "established_by": "identify-deletion-timestamp"
        },
        {
          "description": "backup exists for PITR",
          "fact": "backup_available",
          "established_by": "env"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "reconcile-data",
      "description": "Reconcile any data created after the restore point from application logs",
      "action": "reapply",
      "target": "data",
      "risk_class": "high",
      "rollback": {
        "trigger": "data reconciliation fails",
        "action": "restore database to PITR state",
        "safety_guard": "verify post-reconcile data integrity"
      },
      "verification": {
        "what": "data integrity",
        "how": "compare restored data with application logs",
        "expected": "data is consistent"
      },
      "preconditions": [
        {
          "description": "PITR restore completed successfully",
          "fact": "pitr_success",
          "established_by": "pitr-restore"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "implement-preventive-controls",
      "description": "Implement preventive controls for future data drops",
      "action": "implement",
      "target": "db",
      "risk_class": "medium"
    }
  ],
  "dependencies": [
    {
      "from_task": "stop-writes",
      "to_task": "identify-deletion-timestamp",
      "kind": "hard"
    },
    {
      "from_task": "identify-deletion-timestamp",
      "to_task": "pitr-restore",
      "kind": "hard"
    },
    {
      "from_task": "pitr-restore",
      "to_task": "reconcile-data",
      "kind": "hard"
    }
  ]
}
